1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,431
Rome, the fifteenth of March, in the year we now call 44 BC.

2
00:00:05,811 --> 00:00:06,933
A grey morning.

3
00:00:07,313 --> 00:00:12,326
In a house on the Sacred Way, the most powerful man in the world has slept badly.

4
00:00:12,706 --> 00:00:15,639
His wife has woken in tears from a nightmare.

5
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:21,430
The doors of their bedroom, the story goes, swung open in the night by themselves.

6
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:25,546
He is fifty-five years old.

7
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:33,433
He has conquered Gaul, crossed the Rubicon, defeated every army sent against him and made himself dictator for life.

8
00:00:33,813 --> 00:00:38,945
In three days he is due to leave Rome to begin the greatest war of his career.

9
00:00:39,325 --> 00:00:41,705
And he is thinking about staying home today.

10
00:00:44,005 --> 00:00:51,058
At the other end of the city, in the porticoes beside Pompey's great theatre, a group of senators is waiting for him.

11
00:00:51,438 --> 00:00:59,453
Some of them carry daggers hidden in the folds of their togas, or in the cases that normally hold their writing tablets.

12
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:02,441
There are more than sixty of them in the plot.

13
00:01:02,821 --> 00:01:04,593
Many owe him their lives.

14
00:01:06,893 --> 00:01:10,670
What happened that morning is the most famous murder in history.

15
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:21,197
It has been retold for two thousand years, by historians, by poets and by Shakespeare, and the retellings have buried much of what the sources actually say.

16
00:01:23,497 --> 00:01:31,493
This is the story of how the Roman Republic, a state that had governed itself for nearly five hundred years, came to this moment.

17
00:01:31,873 --> 00:01:36,475
Of the men who decided that one man had to die so that it could survive.

18
00:01:36,855 --> 00:01:41,343
And of how their daggers produced exactly what they were meant to prevent.

19
00:02:00,643 --> 00:02:07,305
To understand why Caesar died, you have to understand what the men who killed him believed they were defending.

20
00:02:07,685 --> 00:02:09,997
Rome had once been ruled by kings.

21
00:02:10,377 --> 00:02:21,074
According to Roman tradition, the last of them, Tarquin the Proud, was driven out in 509 BC by a nobleman named Lucius Junius Brutus.

22
00:02:21,454 --> 00:02:26,123
From then on, the Romans swore, no one would ever again be king in Rome.

23
00:02:28,423 --> 00:02:34,187
In place of a king they built a republic, from the Latin res publica, the public thing.

24
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:40,187
Every year the people elected two consuls to lead the state and command its armies.

25
00:02:40,567 --> 00:02:44,353
Each held power for just twelve months, and each could block the other.

26
00:02:44,733 --> 00:02:50,302
Below them came a ladder of other offices, all elected, all held for a single year.

27
00:02:52,602 --> 00:02:57,784
At the centre stood the Senate, some three hundred former magistrates who served for life.

28
00:02:58,164 --> 00:03:01,174
The Senate had no formal power to make laws.

29
00:03:01,554 --> 00:03:11,981
But its advice was rarely ignored, and its members, a few hundred families who intermarried, competed and remembered every insult for generations, ran Rome.

30
00:03:14,281 --> 00:03:16,739
The system was built for a city state.

31
00:03:17,119 --> 00:03:23,453
But by the first century BC, Rome ruled the Mediterranean, from Spain to Asia Minor.

32
00:03:23,833 --> 00:03:28,295
Its generals commanded armies far from home for years at a time.

33
00:03:28,675 --> 00:03:32,245
Its governors returned from the provinces with fortunes.

34
00:03:32,625 --> 00:03:39,230
And the soldiers who won its wars came to look to their generals, not to the Senate, for land and reward.

35
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:44,503
The century before Caesar was soaked in blood.

36
00:03:44,883 --> 00:03:53,338
In 133 BC, a reforming tribune named Tiberius Gracchus was clubbed to death by senators in the street.

37
00:03:53,718 --> 00:03:58,711
Twelve years later, his brother Gaius died in another outbreak of violence.

38
00:03:59,091 --> 00:04:01,073
Then the generals took over.

39
00:04:01,453 --> 00:04:04,703
Gaius Marius held the consulship seven times.

40
00:04:05,083 --> 00:04:08,633
His rival Sulla did something no Roman had ever done.

41
00:04:09,013 --> 00:04:11,301
He marched his army on Rome itself.

42
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:16,327
Behind the violence was a simple problem.

43
00:04:16,707 --> 00:04:25,924
Rome's wars had flooded Italy with slaves and with wealth, and the great landowners bought up the small farms that had once supplied the legions.

44
00:04:26,304 --> 00:04:29,091
Gracchus had tried to give land back to the poor.

45
00:04:29,471 --> 00:04:33,099
The senators who killed him saw it as a bid for kingship.

46
00:04:33,479 --> 00:04:39,304
That word, king, would be thrown at every popular politician for the next hundred years.

47
00:04:41,604 --> 00:04:52,449
Sulla revived the dictatorship, an emergency office traditionally limited to six months, took it with no time limit at all, and used it to publish lists of his enemies.

48
00:04:52,829 --> 00:04:59,071
Anyone named could be killed by anyone, and the killer was rewarded with part of the victim's property.

49
00:04:59,451 --> 00:05:01,191
These were the proscriptions.

50
00:05:01,571 --> 00:05:02,909
Thousands died.

51
00:05:03,289 --> 00:05:06,873
Rome had learned that the man with the army could do anything.

52
00:05:09,173 --> 00:05:17,307
And among the young men who had to hide from Sulla's death squads was an eighteen-year-old nobleman from an old but faded family.

53
00:05:17,687 --> 00:05:20,095
His name was Gaius Julius Caesar.

54
00:05:32,395 --> 00:05:35,592
The Julii were one of the oldest families in Rome.

55
00:05:35,972 --> 00:05:42,394
They claimed descent from the goddess Venus, through Aeneas, the Trojan hero, and his son Iulus.

56
00:05:42,774 --> 00:05:52,232
But by the time Gaius Julius Caesar was born, in July of the year 100 BC, the family had produced few consuls for generations.

57
00:05:52,612 --> 00:06:00,638
They lived not on the Palatine with the great houses, but in the Subura, a crowded, noisy working district at the foot of the hills.

58
00:06:02,938 --> 00:06:05,710
The family's politics were set by marriage.

59
00:06:06,090 --> 00:06:10,907
Caesar's aunt Julia was the wife of Gaius Marius, the great general.

60
00:06:11,287 --> 00:06:17,116
And at about seventeen, Caesar married Cornelia, the daughter of Marius's ally Cinna.

61
00:06:17,496 --> 00:06:20,757
When Sulla won the civil war, that made him an enemy.

62
00:06:21,137 --> 00:06:23,307
Sulla ordered him to divorce her.

63
00:06:23,687 --> 00:06:25,007
Caesar refused.

64
00:06:27,307 --> 00:06:38,601
He lost his priesthood and his wife's dowry, and he had to go into hiding, moving from house to house in the Sabine country, sick with fever, bribing the patrols that hunted him.

65
00:06:38,981 --> 00:06:44,519
In the end, relatives and the Vestal Virgins pleaded for him, and Sulla gave way.

66
00:06:44,899 --> 00:06:51,276
But, according to Suetonius, he warned them that the boy they were saving would one day destroy them all.

67
00:06:51,656 --> 00:06:54,627
In this Caesar, he said, there are many Mariuses.

68
00:06:56,927 --> 00:07:00,813
Then came the story that the Romans loved to tell about him.

69
00:07:01,193 --> 00:07:07,694
In his mid-twenties, sailing to the island of Rhodes to study rhetoric, he was captured by pirates.

70
00:07:08,074 --> 00:07:14,836
When they told him his ransom would be twenty talents, he laughed at them, and told them he was worth at least fifty.

71
00:07:17,136 --> 00:07:21,482
For more than a month he lived among them while his friends raised the money.

72
00:07:21,862 --> 00:07:33,298
He joined their games, read them his poems and speeches, called them illiterate barbarians when they didn't applaud, and promised, cheerfully, that he would come back and crucify them all.

73
00:07:33,677 --> 00:07:35,363
They thought it was a joke.

74
00:07:35,743 --> 00:07:41,431
When he was freed, he raised a small fleet, captured them and did exactly what he had promised.

75
00:07:43,731 --> 00:07:51,947
The details come from Plutarch and Suetonius, writing a century and a half later, and the story was surely polished in the telling.

76
00:07:52,327 --> 00:07:55,499
But it captures something every source agrees on.

77
00:07:55,879 --> 00:08:00,686
Caesar was charming, fearless, and absolutely certain of his own destiny.

78
00:08:02,986 --> 00:08:05,000
He was also hugely in debt.

79
00:08:05,380 --> 00:08:11,418
To win votes, he borrowed fortunes and spent them on games, gladiators and public feasts.

80
00:08:11,798 --> 00:08:21,010
In 63 BC, he stood for the post of Pontifex Maximus, chief priest of Rome, against two of the most senior men in the Senate.

81
00:08:21,390 --> 00:08:26,927
That morning, he told his mother that he would come home either as high priest or not at all.

82
00:08:27,307 --> 00:08:28,041
He won.

83
00:08:30,341 --> 00:08:38,787
As Pontifex Maximus he moved into the Domus Publica, the official house on the Sacred Way, in the very heart of Rome.

84
00:08:39,167 --> 00:08:45,120
It was the house he would leave on the morning of the Ides of March, nearly twenty years later.

85
00:08:57,420 --> 00:09:01,953
Caesar's rise needed allies, and in 60 BC he found two.

86
00:09:02,333 --> 00:09:13,467
Gnaeus Pompeius, Pompey the Great, was the most famous general in Rome, fresh from conquering the East, and furious that the Senate was refusing to reward his veterans.

87
00:09:13,847 --> 00:09:19,727
Marcus Licinius Crassus was the richest man in Rome, and Caesar's biggest creditor.

88
00:09:20,107 --> 00:09:22,156
The two men loathed each other.

89
00:09:22,536 --> 00:09:24,914
Caesar persuaded them to work together.

90
00:09:27,214 --> 00:09:30,942
Modern historians call their pact the First Triumvirate.

91
00:09:31,322 --> 00:09:36,621
At the time it was simply a private deal between three men to share out the Republic.

92
00:09:37,001 --> 00:09:42,712
With their money and votes behind him, Caesar was elected consul for 59 BC.

93
00:09:43,092 --> 00:09:52,985
His colleague Bibulus tried to block his laws, was driven from the Forum with a basket of dung tipped over his head, and stayed home for the rest of the year.

94
00:09:53,365 --> 00:09:57,355
Romans joked that it was the consulship of Julius and Caesar.

95
00:09:59,655 --> 00:10:03,654
To seal the alliance, Pompey married Caesar's daughter, Julia.

96
00:10:04,034 --> 00:10:07,280
By every account, it became a real love match.

97
00:10:07,660 --> 00:10:14,776
And Caesar got what he wanted most: command of the provinces of Gaul for five years, later extended to ten.

98
00:10:15,156 --> 00:10:18,539
He was forty-one, with debts that only a war could pay.

99
00:10:18,919 --> 00:10:20,377
Gaul would provide it.

100
00:10:22,677 --> 00:10:25,039
The Gallic War lasted eight years.

101
00:10:25,419 --> 00:10:32,639
Caesar fought the Helvetii, the Germans of Ariovistus, the Belgae, and the Veneti on the Atlantic coast.

102
00:10:33,019 --> 00:10:40,896
He bridged the Rhine in ten days and twice crossed the sea to Britain, a land most Romans thought lay at the edge of the world.

103
00:10:43,196 --> 00:10:50,268
In 52 BC the Gauls rose together under a young Arvernian chieftain named Vercingetorix.

104
00:10:50,648 --> 00:11:00,870
Caesar trapped him in the hill town of Alesia and built two rings of fortifications around it, one facing in, the other facing out against a relief army.

105
00:11:01,250 --> 00:11:07,152
When the siege ended, Vercingetorix rode out and laid down his weapons at Caesar's feet.

106
00:11:07,532 --> 00:11:12,978
He was kept prisoner for six years, paraded in Caesar's triumph, and then strangled.

107
00:11:15,278 --> 00:11:24,096
Caesar told the story himself, in his Commentaries, written in plain, clear Latin and in the third person, as if Caesar were someone else.

108
00:11:24,476 --> 00:11:27,048
They are brilliant, and they are propaganda.

109
00:11:27,428 --> 00:11:35,207
According to Pliny the Elder, Caesar counted more than one million one hundred and ninety thousand enemy dead in his wars.

110
00:11:35,587 --> 00:11:38,666
Historians suspect the figure is inflated.

111
00:11:39,046 --> 00:11:45,480
But the slaughter and enslavement were enormous, and the profits made Caesar one of the richest men alive.

112
00:11:47,780 --> 00:11:50,497
While he was away, the alliance fell apart.

113
00:11:50,877 --> 00:11:58,596
In 54 BC Julia died in childbirth, and the bond between her father and her husband died with her.

114
00:11:58,976 --> 00:12:06,627
The next year Crassus led an army against the Parthians in the East and was killed at Carrhae, his legions destroyed.

115
00:12:07,007 --> 00:12:09,018
Now there were only two men left.

116
00:12:09,398 --> 00:12:11,457
And there was room for only one.

117
00:12:23,757 --> 00:12:27,947
By 50 BC, Caesar's enemies in the Senate had a plan.

118
00:12:28,327 --> 00:12:31,564
His command in Gaul gave him legal immunity.

119
00:12:31,944 --> 00:12:41,262
The moment he gave it up and returned to Rome as a private citizen, they would prosecute him for the illegal acts of his consulship and finish his career.

120
00:12:41,642 --> 00:12:48,798
Caesar's answer was to stand for a second consulship without coming home, moving straight from one office to the next.

121
00:12:49,178 --> 00:12:53,591
His enemies, led by the stern Marcus Porcius Cato, refused.

122
00:12:55,891 --> 00:13:01,586
And Pompey, who had once been Caesar's partner and son-in-law, now sided with the Senate.

123
00:13:01,966 --> 00:13:08,241
On the first of January, 49 BC, the Senate ordered Caesar to lay down his command.

124
00:13:08,621 --> 00:13:15,030
Two tribunes who supported him, one of them a young officer named Mark Antony, vetoed the decree.

125
00:13:15,410 --> 00:13:19,213
They were threatened, and fled the city disguised as slaves.

126
00:13:19,593 --> 00:13:24,963
The Senate passed its emergency decree, calling on the magistrates to defend the state.

127
00:13:25,343 --> 00:13:27,522
It was a declaration of war.

128
00:13:29,822 --> 00:13:34,668
Caesar was at Ravenna, near the border of his province, with a single legion.

129
00:13:35,048 --> 00:13:37,968
The border was a small river called the Rubicon.

130
00:13:38,348 --> 00:13:45,939
Under Roman law, a general who led his army across it into Italy without permission became an enemy of the state.

131
00:13:46,319 --> 00:13:57,438
On the night of the tenth of January, he quietly left a dinner party, took a carriage pulled by mules from a nearby bakery, got lost in the dark, and reached the river at dawn.

132
00:13:59,738 --> 00:14:02,976
What happened at the riverbank is told in different ways.

133
00:14:03,356 --> 00:14:08,605
Suetonius says he hesitated, and then said, in Latin, the die is cast.

134
00:14:08,985 --> 00:14:18,630
Plutarch says he spoke the words in Greek, quoting a line from the playwright Menander, and that he spent a long time going over the consequences before he crossed.

135
00:14:19,010 --> 00:14:21,478
Every version agrees on one thing.

136
00:14:21,858 --> 00:14:22,745
He crossed.

137
00:14:25,045 --> 00:14:28,782
Nobody today is certain which river was the Rubicon.

138
00:14:29,162 --> 00:14:32,000
Courses have shifted over two thousand years.

139
00:14:32,380 --> 00:14:38,483
The stream that now bears the name was officially designated only in the twentieth century.

140
00:14:38,863 --> 00:14:44,034
But the phrase crossing the Rubicon has meant the point of no return ever since.

141
00:14:46,334 --> 00:14:48,705
Italy fell almost without a fight.

142
00:14:49,085 --> 00:14:51,640
Town after town opened its gates.

143
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:59,760
Pompey, who had boasted that he only had to stamp his foot for armies to rise out of the ground, found that he had no army ready.

144
00:15:00,140 --> 00:15:09,367
He abandoned Rome and retreated south to Brundisium, and in March he sailed across the Adriatic to Greece, taking most of the Senate with him.

145
00:15:11,667 --> 00:15:13,528
Caesar surprised everyone.

146
00:15:13,908 --> 00:15:16,647
Sulla had answered victory with death lists.

147
00:15:17,027 --> 00:15:24,195
Caesar pardoned the enemy officers he captured and let them go, even when he knew they would fight against him again.

148
00:15:24,575 --> 00:15:26,490
He called it his clemency.

149
00:15:26,870 --> 00:15:30,552
His enemies understood the insult hidden inside it.

150
00:15:30,932 --> 00:15:35,525
To be pardoned by Caesar was to admit that your life was his to give.

151
00:15:47,825 --> 00:15:51,318
The war that followed spread across the whole Roman world.

152
00:15:51,698 --> 00:15:53,596
Caesar secured Spain first.

153
00:15:53,976 --> 00:16:03,611
Then, in the winter of 49 BC, he crossed the Adriatic in pursuit of Pompey, slipping past the enemy fleet with only part of his army.

154
00:16:03,991 --> 00:16:09,195
At Dyrrhachium, on the coast of what is now Albania, Pompey beat him badly.

155
00:16:09,575 --> 00:16:17,112
Caesar is said to have remarked that the war would have been lost that day if the other side had had a commander who knew how to win.

156
00:16:19,412 --> 00:16:24,249
He retreated inland to Thessaly, in northern Greece, and Pompey followed him.

157
00:16:24,629 --> 00:16:30,423
On the ninth of August, 48 BC, the two armies met on the plain of Pharsalus.

158
00:16:30,803 --> 00:16:34,768
Pompey had perhaps twice as many men, and far more cavalry.

159
00:16:35,148 --> 00:16:43,548
The senators in his camp were so sure of victory that they had already started arguing over who would get Caesar's offices and houses.

160
00:16:45,848 --> 00:16:49,722
Caesar had expected Pompey's cavalry to sweep round his flank.

161
00:16:50,102 --> 00:16:53,935
Behind his lines he hid a fourth line of veteran infantry.

162
00:16:54,315 --> 00:17:01,474
When the horsemen charged, these men rose up and drove their spears at the riders, and the cavalry broke and fled.

163
00:17:01,854 --> 00:17:03,522
Pompey's army collapsed.

164
00:17:03,902 --> 00:17:09,400
Pompey himself rode back to his camp, sat in his tent in silence, and then fled.

165
00:17:11,700 --> 00:17:19,656
Walking across the battlefield afterwards, among thousands of dead Romans, Caesar is reported to have said, they wanted this.

166
00:17:20,036 --> 00:17:26,774
After all I had done, I, Gaius Caesar, would have been condemned if I had not turned to my army for help.

167
00:17:27,154 --> 00:17:34,752
Among the enemy soldiers who surrendered that day and were pardoned was a young officer named Marcus Junius Brutus.

168
00:17:37,052 --> 00:17:40,935
Pompey's army had been made up of Romans, and so had Caesar's.

169
00:17:41,315 --> 00:17:43,600
This was the cost of civil war.

170
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:51,281
According to Caesar's own account, about fifteen thousand of Pompey's men died and twenty-four thousand surrendered.

171
00:17:51,661 --> 00:17:56,878
He lost, he claims, only two hundred men, though thirty of them were centurions.

172
00:17:57,258 --> 00:18:00,298
Historians treat these figures with suspicion.

173
00:18:00,678 --> 00:18:02,946
Caesar was the only one counting.

174
00:18:05,246 --> 00:18:14,124
Pompey fled east, and then to Egypt, where he hoped for protection from the young king Ptolemy the Thirteenth, whose father he had once helped.

175
00:18:14,504 --> 00:18:17,259
The boy king's advisers did their sums.

176
00:18:17,639 --> 00:18:30,948
On the twenty-eighth of September, as Pompey stepped from a small boat onto the beach at Pelusium, in full view of his wife on the ship behind him, he was stabbed to death by a Roman officer who had once served under him.

177
00:18:33,248 --> 00:18:36,619
A few days later, Caesar arrived at Alexandria.

178
00:18:36,999 --> 00:18:44,340
The Egyptians brought him a gift: Pompey's head, and his signet ring, engraved with a lion holding a sword.

179
00:18:44,720 --> 00:18:50,741
According to Plutarch, Caesar turned away from the head in disgust and wept when he took the ring.

180
00:18:51,121 --> 00:18:56,265
The man who had been his ally, his son-in-law, and finally his enemy, was dead.

181
00:18:56,645 --> 00:18:58,749
Caesar was now alone at the top.

182
00:19:11,049 --> 00:19:14,146
Egypt was in the middle of its own civil war.

183
00:19:14,526 --> 00:19:28,021
The boy king Ptolemy shared his throne with his elder sister, Cleopatra the Seventh, twenty-one years old, clever, ruthless, and the first of her Greek-speaking dynasty said to have learned the Egyptian language.

184
00:19:28,401 --> 00:19:32,264
Her brother's advisers had driven her out of Alexandria.

185
00:19:32,644 --> 00:19:37,279
Caesar, who wanted Egypt's money, announced that he would settle the dispute.

186
00:19:39,579 --> 00:19:44,064
Cleopatra needed to reach him without being caught by her brother's men.

187
00:19:44,444 --> 00:19:54,006
According to Plutarch, a single friend rowed her across the harbour at dusk, tied her inside a bedding sack, and carried her into the palace on his shoulder.

188
00:19:54,386 --> 00:19:57,408
The famous carpet comes from later retellings.

189
00:19:57,788 --> 00:20:02,724
Either way, Caesar was captivated, and within days he had taken her side.

190
00:20:05,024 --> 00:20:07,546
The Alexandrians rose against him.

191
00:20:07,926 --> 00:20:13,324
For months Caesar was besieged in the palace quarter with only a few thousand men.

192
00:20:13,704 --> 00:20:23,862
During the fighting, fire spread from the burning ships in the harbour, and some ancient writers say that part of the great Library of Alexandria went up in flames.

193
00:20:24,242 --> 00:20:26,843
How much was lost is still debated.

194
00:20:27,223 --> 00:20:34,529
At one point Caesar had to jump into the harbour and swim for his life, holding his papers above the water.

195
00:20:36,829 --> 00:20:40,914
In the spring of 47 BC, reinforcements arrived.

196
00:20:41,294 --> 00:20:49,321
The young king Ptolemy drowned in the Nile while fleeing the battle, and Cleopatra was placed on the throne with a younger brother.

197
00:20:49,701 --> 00:20:55,340
Caesar stayed on in Egypt for weeks, and the sources say he sailed with the queen up the Nile.

198
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:58,590
That summer, Cleopatra gave birth to a son.

199
00:20:58,970 --> 00:21:01,578
She called him Caesarion, little Caesar.

200
00:21:01,958 --> 00:21:07,258
Whether he was really Caesar's child was argued over then and is argued over still.

201
00:21:09,558 --> 00:21:11,437
Then Caesar went back to war.

202
00:21:11,817 --> 00:21:17,133
In Asia Minor, King Pharnaces of Pontus had taken advantage of the chaos.

203
00:21:17,513 --> 00:21:21,687
Caesar met him at Zela and destroyed his army in about four hours.

204
00:21:22,067 --> 00:21:25,270
He reported the campaign to Rome in three words.

205
00:21:25,650 --> 00:21:27,352
Veni, vidi, vici.

206
00:21:27,732 --> 00:21:29,701
I came, I saw, I conquered.

207
00:21:32,001 --> 00:21:38,116
In Africa the next year, at Thapsus, he destroyed the last great army of the Senate's party.

208
00:21:38,496 --> 00:21:42,892
Cato, rather than accept Caesar's pardon, killed himself at Utica.

209
00:21:43,272 --> 00:21:50,555
When Caesar heard, he said he begrudged Cato his death, because Cato had begrudged him the chance to save his life.

210
00:21:50,935 --> 00:21:58,382
Back in Rome, Caesar celebrated four triumphs in a single month: over Gaul, Egypt, Pontus and Africa.

211
00:21:58,762 --> 00:22:03,619
Vercingetorix was led in chains through the streets and then put to death.

212
00:22:05,919 --> 00:22:07,353
There was one more war.

213
00:22:07,733 --> 00:22:10,768
Pompey's sons raised a new army in Spain.

214
00:22:11,148 --> 00:22:19,096
At Munda, in March 45 BC, the battle went so badly for Caesar that he plunged into the front line himself.

215
00:22:19,476 --> 00:22:25,774
He said afterwards that he had often fought for victory, but this was the first time he had fought for his life.

216
00:22:26,154 --> 00:22:26,888
He won.

217
00:22:27,268 --> 00:22:28,995
The civil war was over.

218
00:22:41,295 --> 00:22:44,556
Caesar now held more power than any Roman before him.

219
00:22:44,936 --> 00:22:52,768
He was named dictator, first for ten years, and then, in early 44 BC, for life: dictator perpetuo.

220
00:22:53,148 --> 00:22:56,334
He was consul as well, whenever he chose to be.

221
00:22:56,714 --> 00:23:00,775
Magistrates were elected, but they were the men he recommended.

222
00:23:01,155 --> 00:23:04,957
The Senate still met, but the decisions were made in his house.

223
00:23:07,257 --> 00:23:08,290
And he used it.

224
00:23:08,670 --> 00:23:15,855
In just a few years he reformed the calendar, which had drifted by about three months out of step with the seasons.

225
00:23:16,236 --> 00:23:21,843
To fix it, the year 46 BC was stretched to four hundred and forty-five days.

226
00:23:22,223 --> 00:23:31,972
From the first of January, 45 BC, Rome used a new calendar of three hundred and sixty-five days, with a leap day every four years.

227
00:23:32,352 --> 00:23:36,397
With one later adjustment, it is the calendar we still use.

228
00:23:38,697 --> 00:23:47,142
He enlarged the Senate to about nine hundred members, filling it with his own supporters, including provincials and soldiers' sons.

229
00:23:47,522 --> 00:23:58,386
He settled tens of thousands of veterans and poor citizens in new colonies abroad, and refounded Carthage and Corinth, cities Rome had destroyed a century before.

230
00:23:58,766 --> 00:24:07,845
He planned public libraries, a new theatre, a vast temple to Mars, the draining of marshes, a canal through the Isthmus of Corinth.

231
00:24:10,145 --> 00:24:20,427
His new Forum rose beside the old one, dominated by a temple to Venus Genetrix, Venus the Mother, the goddess from whom his family claimed descent.

232
00:24:20,807 --> 00:24:26,505
Inside, next to the statue of the goddess, he placed a gilded statue of Cleopatra.

233
00:24:26,885 --> 00:24:30,871
The queen herself was living in his villa across the Tiber.

234
00:24:33,171 --> 00:24:34,593
The honours piled up.

235
00:24:34,973 --> 00:24:39,409
His statue was set up on the Capitol beside those of the ancient kings.

236
00:24:39,789 --> 00:24:44,095
The month of his birth, Quintilis, was renamed Julius, July.

237
00:24:44,475 --> 00:24:49,913
His face appeared on Roman coins, the first living Roman ever given that honour.

238
00:24:50,293 --> 00:24:56,523
He was allowed to wear the purple robe and laurel wreath of a triumphing general whenever he liked.

239
00:24:56,903 --> 00:25:03,809
Suetonius notes, a little unkindly, that he was pleased by the wreath, because it hid his bald patch.

240
00:25:06,109 --> 00:25:09,413
Then came the moments that his enemies never forgot.

241
00:25:09,793 --> 00:25:19,446
When a delegation of senators came to him at the Temple of Venus Genetrix to report the latest honours voted to him, he did not stand up to greet them.

242
00:25:19,826 --> 00:25:22,030
To the Senate it was an insult.

243
00:25:22,410 --> 00:25:24,431
Some sources say he was unwell.

244
00:25:24,811 --> 00:25:30,235
Others say he was about to rise when a friend told him to remember that he was Caesar.

245
00:25:32,535 --> 00:25:45,269
At the festival of the Lupercalia, on the fifteenth of February, 44 BC, the consul Mark Antony ran up to him in the Forum in front of a huge crowd and offered him a royal diadem.

246
00:25:45,649 --> 00:25:47,053
Caesar refused it.

247
00:25:47,433 --> 00:25:49,160
Antony offered it again.

248
00:25:49,540 --> 00:25:52,588
Caesar refused again, and the crowd cheered.

249
00:25:52,968 --> 00:25:56,652
He ordered it to be recorded that he had refused the crown.

250
00:25:57,032 --> 00:25:58,179
Was it a test?

251
00:25:58,559 --> 00:26:00,061
A piece of theatre?

252
00:26:00,441 --> 00:26:01,220
A trap?

253
00:26:01,600 --> 00:26:02,726
Nobody knows.

254
00:26:03,106 --> 00:26:08,167
But in a city whose founding oath was never again a king, the question had been asked.

255
00:26:20,467 --> 00:26:21,545
Rumours spread.

256
00:26:21,925 --> 00:26:32,132
People whispered that Caesar wanted to be king, that he planned to move the capital to Alexandria, that a law was being drafted to let him marry as many wives as he wished.

257
00:26:32,512 --> 00:26:34,231
Most of it was gossip.

258
00:26:34,611 --> 00:26:38,611
But one rumour, reported by Suetonius, was more specific.

259
00:26:38,991 --> 00:26:46,273
An ancient prophecy in the Sibylline Books was said to declare that the Parthians could only be defeated by a king.

260
00:26:46,653 --> 00:26:49,777
And Caesar was about to march against the Parthians.

261
00:26:52,077 --> 00:26:59,897
Late at night, statues of the ancient Brutus, the man who had driven out the last king, were found covered with messages.

262
00:27:00,277 --> 00:27:01,886
Would that you were alive.

263
00:27:02,266 --> 00:27:04,672
Your descendants are unworthy of you.

264
00:27:05,052 --> 00:27:14,838
And on the official seat of his descendant, Marcus Junius Brutus, now one of Rome's chief magistrates, someone wrote: Brutus, you are asleep.

265
00:27:15,218 --> 00:27:16,947
You are not really Brutus.

266
00:27:19,247 --> 00:27:24,063
Brutus was forty-one, serious, bookish, admired for his integrity.

267
00:27:24,443 --> 00:27:28,006
He had fought against Caesar at Pharsalus and been pardoned.

268
00:27:28,386 --> 00:27:32,503
Since then Caesar had promoted him, and planned to make him consul.

269
00:27:32,883 --> 00:27:39,732
His mother, Servilia, had for years been Caesar's lover, which led to a rumour that Brutus was Caesar's son.

270
00:27:40,112 --> 00:27:42,063
It is almost certainly untrue.

271
00:27:42,443 --> 00:27:45,828
Caesar would have been about fifteen when Brutus was born.

272
00:27:48,128 --> 00:27:53,295
The man who recruited him was his brother-in-law, Gaius Cassius Longinus.

273
00:27:53,674 --> 00:28:02,003
Cassius was a hard, proud soldier who had saved what was left of Crassus's army after Carrhae, and who had also been pardoned.

274
00:28:02,383 --> 00:28:04,304
He had a temper, and a grudge.

275
00:28:04,684 --> 00:28:13,002
Plutarch says Caesar distrusted him, and once remarked, of Cassius and Brutus, that he did not fear the fat, long-haired men.

276
00:28:13,382 --> 00:28:16,339
It was the pale, thin ones he worried about.

277
00:28:18,639 --> 00:28:28,246
The third key man is less famous but may have been the most important of all: Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus, a distant cousin of Marcus.

278
00:28:28,626 --> 00:28:31,637
He had been one of Caesar's best generals in Gaul.

279
00:28:32,017 --> 00:28:40,214
Caesar trusted him completely, named him governor of a province, and, as the conspirators did not know, had listed him in his will.

280
00:28:40,594 --> 00:28:50,378
Other conspirators were Gaius Trebonius, another of Caesar's old officers, Publius Servilius Casca and his brother, and Lucius Tillius Cimber.

281
00:28:52,677 --> 00:28:55,142
In the end there were more than sixty of them.

282
00:28:55,522 --> 00:28:58,276
Some were old Pompeians nursing their pride.

283
00:28:58,656 --> 00:29:03,552
Some were Caesar's own officers who felt cheated of the rewards they expected.

284
00:29:03,932 --> 00:29:08,947
Some, like Brutus, truly believed they were saving the Republic from a tyrant.

285
00:29:09,327 --> 00:29:11,738
Remarkably, the plot never leaked.

286
00:29:12,118 --> 00:29:16,014
Plutarch says no one swore an oath, and no one betrayed it.

287
00:29:18,314 --> 00:29:23,729
They deliberately left out the most famous orator in Rome, Marcus Tullius Cicero.

288
00:29:24,109 --> 00:29:27,143
He shared their views, but they doubted his nerve.

289
00:29:27,523 --> 00:29:32,569
And they argued about Mark Antony, Caesar's loyal deputy and fellow consul.

290
00:29:32,949 --> 00:29:35,022
Cassius wanted to kill him too.

291
00:29:35,402 --> 00:29:36,812
Brutus refused.

292
00:29:37,192 --> 00:29:42,313
This was to be an act of justice against a tyrant, he said, not a massacre.

293
00:29:42,693 --> 00:29:45,572
It was a decision that would cost them everything.

294
00:29:57,872 --> 00:30:00,003
The conspirators had a deadline.

295
00:30:00,383 --> 00:30:10,134
On the eighteenth of March, Caesar was due to leave Rome for the East, to fight a war against Dacia and then against Parthia that might last three years.

296
00:30:10,514 --> 00:30:13,000
Sixteen legions were being prepared.

297
00:30:13,380 --> 00:30:19,577
Once he was with his army, surrounded by soldiers who worshipped him, no one would be able to touch him.

298
00:30:19,957 --> 00:30:23,293
If they were going to act, it had to be before he left.

299
00:30:25,593 --> 00:30:27,306
They considered the options.

300
00:30:27,686 --> 00:30:30,724
Attack him on the Sacred Way as he walked home?

301
00:30:31,104 --> 00:30:34,900
At the elections, as he stood on the bridge where voters crossed?

302
00:30:35,280 --> 00:30:36,894
At the gladiator games?

303
00:30:37,274 --> 00:30:40,119
In the end they chose a meeting of the Senate.

304
00:30:40,499 --> 00:30:45,684
Only senators could attend, so his friends and bodyguards would have to stay outside.

305
00:30:46,064 --> 00:30:51,547
And the conspirators could carry daggers hidden in their togas without arousing suspicion.

306
00:30:51,927 --> 00:30:57,498
If anything went wrong, Decimus had a troupe of gladiators at the theatre nearby.

307
00:30:59,798 --> 00:31:04,032
The Senate was due to meet on the Ides of March, the fifteenth.

308
00:31:04,412 --> 00:31:13,332
Its usual home, the old Senate house in the Forum, had burned down eight years before, and the new one Caesar was building was not finished.

309
00:31:13,712 --> 00:31:26,211
So this meeting would be held in a hall attached to the great complex built by his dead rival: the Curia of Pompey, at the end of a long garden portico behind Pompey's theatre.

310
00:31:26,591 --> 00:31:28,803
A statue of Pompey stood inside.

311
00:31:31,103 --> 00:31:32,822
Did Caesar suspect?

312
00:31:33,202 --> 00:31:35,304
He had been warned many times.

313
00:31:35,684 --> 00:31:38,109
He had dismissed his Spanish bodyguard.

314
00:31:38,489 --> 00:31:46,781
When friends urged him to protect himself, he is said to have answered that it was better to die once than to be always afraid of dying.

315
00:31:47,161 --> 00:31:52,074
He also argued that his survival was in the Republic's interest, more than his own.

316
00:31:52,454 --> 00:31:58,622
If anything happened to him, he said, Rome would be plunged into civil wars far worse than before.

317
00:32:00,922 --> 00:32:04,125
The conspirators were not the only ones under strain.

318
00:32:04,505 --> 00:32:06,373
Brutus could hardly sleep.

319
00:32:06,753 --> 00:32:11,312
His wife, Porcia, the daughter of Cato, guessed that something was wrong.

320
00:32:11,692 --> 00:32:22,011
According to Plutarch, to prove that she could keep a secret, she cut deep into her own thigh with a small knife, hid the wound for a day, and only then showed him.

321
00:32:22,391 --> 00:32:24,330
Brutus told her everything.

322
00:32:26,630 --> 00:32:31,177
There were omens too, or at least the ancient writers later remembered them.

323
00:32:31,557 --> 00:32:41,486
A soothsayer named Spurinna, reading the entrails of a sacrifice, warned Caesar of great danger that would come no later than the Ides of March.

324
00:32:41,866 --> 00:32:48,693
The horses that Caesar had released after crossing the Rubicon were said to be refusing to eat, and weeping.

325
00:32:49,073 --> 00:32:58,992
And on the day before the Ides, a small bird flew into the Curia of Pompey carrying a sprig of laurel, chased by other birds, who tore it to pieces.

326
00:33:01,292 --> 00:33:07,749
On the evening of the fourteenth of March, Caesar dined at the house of his deputy, Marcus Lepidus.

327
00:33:08,129 --> 00:33:12,540
The talk turned to death, and someone asked what kind of death was the best.

328
00:33:12,920 --> 00:33:16,543
Before anyone else could answer, Caesar said: a sudden one.

329
00:33:28,843 --> 00:33:32,701
That night, Caesar's wife Calpurnia dreamed of disaster.

330
00:33:33,081 --> 00:33:36,719
In one version, she held his murdered body in her arms.

331
00:33:37,099 --> 00:33:43,060
In another, the ornamental gable that the Senate had voted to decorate his house had collapsed.

332
00:33:43,440 --> 00:33:46,930
She woke in terror and begged him not to go out that day.

333
00:33:47,310 --> 00:33:50,656
Caesar, who had never been superstitious, was shaken.

334
00:33:51,036 --> 00:33:52,928
He was also feeling unwell.

335
00:33:53,308 --> 00:33:57,051
He decided to send Mark Antony to dismiss the Senate.

336
00:33:59,351 --> 00:34:03,648
The priests had made sacrifices that morning, and the signs were bad.

337
00:34:04,028 --> 00:34:09,177
If Caesar stayed home, the plot was finished, and probably the conspirators too.

338
00:34:09,557 --> 00:34:12,687
So they sent the one man he trusted completely.

339
00:34:13,067 --> 00:34:17,206
Decimus Brutus came to the house, and mocked the omens gently.

340
00:34:17,586 --> 00:34:22,693
Was Caesar really going to make the Senate wait because his wife had had a bad dream?

341
00:34:23,073 --> 00:34:26,072
The senators would think him a tyrant, or a fool.

342
00:34:26,452 --> 00:34:29,172
At least come and dismiss them in person.

343
00:34:31,472 --> 00:34:35,146
It was the late morning by the time Caesar left the house.

344
00:34:35,526 --> 00:34:40,963
He was carried through the streets in a litter, and as always people pressed forward with petitions.

345
00:34:41,343 --> 00:34:48,408
One of them was a Greek teacher of rhetoric named Artemidorus of Cnidos, who knew some of the conspirators.

346
00:34:48,788 --> 00:34:56,640
He pushed his way through and handed Caesar a small scroll, urging him to read it at once, alone, because it concerned him.

347
00:34:57,020 --> 00:34:58,929
Caesar held it in his hand.

348
00:34:59,309 --> 00:35:00,558
He never read it.

349
00:35:02,858 --> 00:35:08,095
Inside the portico of Pompey, the conspirators waited, trying to look normal.

350
00:35:08,475 --> 00:35:12,303
Brutus was presiding as praetor, hearing legal cases.

351
00:35:12,683 --> 00:35:23,464
One of the senators, Popilius Laenas, came up to Brutus and Cassius and whispered that he hoped their business would succeed, and that they should hurry, because people were talking.

352
00:35:23,844 --> 00:35:24,983
They went cold.

353
00:35:25,363 --> 00:35:31,118
When Caesar arrived, Popilius went straight to him and spoke into his ear for a long time.

354
00:35:31,498 --> 00:35:36,655
The conspirators watched, ready, some say, to kill themselves if he revealed the plot.

355
00:35:37,035 --> 00:35:41,107
Then Caesar nodded, and Popilius kissed his hand and stepped back.

356
00:35:43,407 --> 00:35:46,861
Outside the hall, the sacrifices were performed again.

357
00:35:47,241 --> 00:35:48,446
They were bad again.

358
00:35:48,826 --> 00:35:50,591
Caesar went in anyway.

359
00:35:50,971 --> 00:35:59,131
As he passed Spurinna, the soothsayer, he called out to him cheerfully that the Ides of March had come, and nothing had happened.

360
00:35:59,511 --> 00:36:02,236
Spurinna answered: yes, they have come.

361
00:36:02,616 --> 00:36:04,333
But they have not yet gone.

362
00:36:06,633 --> 00:36:15,153
Outside, at the doors, Trebonius, or according to another account Decimus, drew Mark Antony aside and kept him talking.

363
00:36:15,533 --> 00:36:18,441
Antony was strong, loyal and a consul.

364
00:36:18,821 --> 00:36:21,286
He was the one man who might have fought back.

365
00:36:21,666 --> 00:36:22,973
He stayed outside.

366
00:36:25,273 --> 00:36:30,855
Inside, the senators rose as Caesar entered and took his seat on a gilded chair.

367
00:36:31,235 --> 00:36:33,551
There were several hundred men in the hall.

368
00:36:33,931 --> 00:36:37,296
Perhaps sixty of them knew what was about to happen.

369
00:36:49,596 --> 00:36:53,953
The conspirators gathered around his chair, as if to pay their respects.

370
00:36:54,333 --> 00:36:57,140
Tillius Cimber stepped forward with a petition.

371
00:36:57,520 --> 00:37:01,335
His brother was in exile, and he begged Caesar to recall him.

372
00:37:01,715 --> 00:37:07,449
The others joined in, pressing closer, taking Caesar's hands, kissing his chest and head.

373
00:37:07,829 --> 00:37:10,377
Caesar refused, and waved them away.

374
00:37:10,757 --> 00:37:15,783
Then Cimber grabbed his toga with both hands and pulled it down from his shoulders.

375
00:37:18,083 --> 00:37:19,448
That was the signal.

376
00:37:19,828 --> 00:37:22,562
Caesar shouted: Why, this is violence!

377
00:37:22,942 --> 00:37:25,823
Casca, standing behind him, struck first.

378
00:37:26,203 --> 00:37:27,558
His dagger slipped.

379
00:37:27,938 --> 00:37:30,976
The blow landed near the neck, a shallow wound.

380
00:37:31,356 --> 00:37:39,464
Caesar turned and grabbed Casca's arm, and stabbed it with the only weapon he had, the sharp iron stylus he used for writing.

381
00:37:41,764 --> 00:37:44,836
Casca shouted to his brother in Greek for help.

382
00:37:45,216 --> 00:37:46,849
And then they all closed in.

383
00:37:47,229 --> 00:37:49,332
Daggers came from every direction.

384
00:37:49,712 --> 00:37:53,220
In the confusion, the conspirators wounded one another.

385
00:37:53,600 --> 00:37:55,475
Cassius struck at his face.

386
00:37:55,855 --> 00:37:58,047
Brutus was wounded in the hand.

387
00:37:58,427 --> 00:38:04,843
The senators who were not in the plot sat frozen in horror, unable to move, unable to call out.

388
00:38:07,142 --> 00:38:12,301
Caesar fought back, Plutarch says, turning this way and that like a wild animal.

389
00:38:12,680 --> 00:38:16,956
But when he saw Brutus coming at him with a dagger, he stopped resisting.

390
00:38:17,336 --> 00:38:19,651
He pulled his toga up over his head.

391
00:38:20,031 --> 00:38:28,538
With his left hand, Suetonius adds, he drew the folds down over his legs, so that he would fall decently, with his body covered.

392
00:38:30,838 --> 00:38:33,035
He was stabbed twenty-three times.

393
00:38:33,415 --> 00:38:44,928
He fell at the foot of the statue of Pompey, which was splashed with his blood, so that, as Plutarch writes, it seemed that Pompey himself was presiding over the vengeance taken on his enemy.

394
00:38:47,228 --> 00:38:48,709
What were his last words?

395
00:38:49,089 --> 00:38:54,050
Suetonius says that he said nothing at all, but only groaned at the first blow.

396
00:38:54,430 --> 00:39:02,366
Though, he adds, some writers claim that when he saw Marcus Brutus coming at him, he said in Greek: kai su, teknon?

397
00:39:02,746 --> 00:39:04,044
You too, child?

398
00:39:04,424 --> 00:39:07,469
Et tu, Brute? is not in any ancient source.

399
00:39:07,849 --> 00:39:12,128
It comes from the Elizabethan stage, and Shakespeare made it immortal.

400
00:39:14,428 --> 00:39:18,187
Later, a doctor named Antistius examined the body.

401
00:39:18,567 --> 00:39:24,184
Of the twenty-three wounds, he reported, only one was fatal: the second, in the chest.

402
00:39:24,564 --> 00:39:29,568
Another early writer, Nicolaus of Damascus, says there were thirty-five.

403
00:39:29,948 --> 00:39:40,200
Whatever the number, the man who had survived Gaul, the civil war, Alexandria and Munda was dead on the floor of a hall built by the rival he had destroyed.

404
00:39:42,500 --> 00:39:46,313
The whole attack may have lasted no more than a minute or two.

405
00:39:46,693 --> 00:39:53,184
The man who struck first was Casca, a senator whose name is now remembered for almost nothing else.

406
00:39:53,564 --> 00:39:57,049
Most of the conspirators struck only once, if at all.

407
00:39:57,429 --> 00:39:59,463
Some of the blows were symbolic.

408
00:39:59,843 --> 00:40:05,525
The point was that they had all taken part, so that none of them could later claim to be innocent.

409
00:40:17,825 --> 00:40:20,902
Brutus turned to the senators to make a speech.

410
00:40:21,282 --> 00:40:24,293
He wanted to explain what they had done and why.

411
00:40:24,673 --> 00:40:26,808
But there was no one left to hear it.

412
00:40:27,188 --> 00:40:37,335
The senators who were not in the plot had fled the hall in panic, scrambling over the benches, pushing through the doors, spreading the news across the city as they ran.

413
00:40:37,715 --> 00:40:40,346
Rome did not hear a speech about liberty.

414
00:40:40,726 --> 00:40:41,957
It heard screaming.

415
00:40:44,257 --> 00:40:53,647
The assassins marched out together through the streets toward the Capitol, holding up their bloody daggers and calling out that they had killed a king and a tyrant.

416
00:40:54,027 --> 00:41:01,030
One of them carried a felt cap on the end of a spear, the cap given to freed slaves, the symbol of liberty.

417
00:41:01,410 --> 00:41:04,211
They were joined by Decimus's gladiators.

418
00:41:04,591 --> 00:41:06,213
But the people did not cheer.

419
00:41:06,593 --> 00:41:08,067
They shut their doors.

420
00:41:10,367 --> 00:41:13,338
For hours, Caesar's body lay alone in the hall.

421
00:41:13,718 --> 00:41:20,876
Finally, three slaves came, put him in a litter, and carried him home through the streets to the house on the Sacred Way.

422
00:41:21,256 --> 00:41:27,499
The curtains of the litter were drawn back, Suetonius says, and one arm hung down over the side.

423
00:41:29,799 --> 00:41:37,377
Mark Antony, terrified that he was next, had fled home disguised as a slave and barricaded himself in.

424
00:41:37,757 --> 00:41:40,482
But the conspirators had made two mistakes.

425
00:41:40,862 --> 00:41:42,309
They had not killed him.

426
00:41:42,689 --> 00:41:45,189
And they had no plan for what came next.

427
00:41:45,569 --> 00:41:50,924
They had assumed that, with the tyrant gone, the Republic would simply restart itself.

428
00:41:51,304 --> 00:41:57,062
Instead, Antony went to Calpurnia that night and took charge of Caesar's papers and his money.

429
00:41:57,442 --> 00:42:00,963
Caesar's deputy Lepidus brought troops into the Forum.

430
00:42:03,263 --> 00:42:08,758
In the meantime, Brutus spoke to the people from the Capitol, and then again in the Forum.

431
00:42:09,138 --> 00:42:11,179
The crowd listened in silence.

432
00:42:11,559 --> 00:42:14,971
They did not attack him, but they did not join him either.

433
00:42:15,351 --> 00:42:21,003
Many of them were Caesar's veterans, waiting in the city for the land they had been promised.

434
00:42:21,383 --> 00:42:26,410
They wanted to know whether the assassins would honour Caesar's grants, or take them away.

435
00:42:28,710 --> 00:42:35,640
On the seventeenth of March, two days after the murder, the Senate met in the Temple of the Earth goddess, Tellus.

436
00:42:36,020 --> 00:42:38,483
It was a strange, tense compromise.

437
00:42:38,863 --> 00:42:43,226
On Cicero's proposal, the assassins were granted an amnesty.

438
00:42:43,606 --> 00:42:48,720
But Caesar was not declared a tyrant, and all his acts and appointments were confirmed.

439
00:42:49,100 --> 00:42:54,304
That suited almost everyone, because so many senators owed their positions to him.

440
00:42:54,684 --> 00:43:00,610
It also meant that the men who had killed a tyrant now had to live under the tyrant's laws.

441
00:43:02,910 --> 00:43:07,345
Cicero, who had not been told about the plot, was overjoyed at the murder.

442
00:43:07,725 --> 00:43:10,278
But within weeks he saw what had gone wrong.

443
00:43:10,658 --> 00:43:18,304
In a letter to his friend Atticus, he wrote that the assassins had acted with the courage of men, but the judgment of boys.

444
00:43:18,684 --> 00:43:22,566
They had cut down the tree, he complained, but left the roots.

445
00:43:34,866 --> 00:43:39,199
Then Caesar's will was opened and read aloud, at Antony's house.

446
00:43:39,579 --> 00:43:41,234
It changed everything.

447
00:43:41,614 --> 00:43:48,729
To every Roman citizen in the city, Caesar left three hundred sesterces, months of wages for a labourer.

448
00:43:49,109 --> 00:43:53,953
He left his gardens across the Tiber to the people of Rome as a public park.

449
00:43:54,333 --> 00:44:02,630
And as his principal heir he named his great-nephew, an eighteen-year-old most Romans had never heard of, Gaius Octavius.

450
00:44:03,010 --> 00:44:06,139
In a final clause, he adopted him as his son.

451
00:44:08,439 --> 00:44:10,441
There was worse for the assassins.

452
00:44:10,821 --> 00:44:15,769
Among the heirs named in case the first ones failed was Decimus Brutus.

453
00:44:16,149 --> 00:44:20,917
The man who had brought Caesar to the Senate that morning had been named in his will.

454
00:44:21,297 --> 00:44:24,232
When the people heard, the mood in the city turned.

455
00:44:26,532 --> 00:44:31,301
The funeral was held a few days later, probably on the twentieth of March.

456
00:44:31,681 --> 00:44:38,864
A gilded shrine, modelled on the Temple of Venus Genetrix, was set up on the speaker's platform in the Forum.

457
00:44:39,244 --> 00:44:44,503
Inside it lay Caesar's body on an ivory couch draped in purple and gold.

458
00:44:44,883 --> 00:44:48,845
At its head hung the toga he had been wearing when he was killed.

459
00:44:51,145 --> 00:44:54,884
What Mark Antony actually said that day is uncertain.

460
00:44:55,264 --> 00:45:05,204
Suetonius says he made only a brief speech, and had a herald read out the honours the Senate had voted Caesar, and the oath by which they had sworn to protect him.

461
00:45:05,584 --> 00:45:15,104
The later historian Appian gives him a long, passionate oration, and says that he held up the bloodstained, slashed toga on the point of a spear.

462
00:45:15,484 --> 00:45:20,465
The great speech that begins, Friends, Romans, countrymen, is Shakespeare's.

463
00:45:22,765 --> 00:45:25,914
Appian also describes something extraordinary.

464
00:45:26,294 --> 00:45:36,258
Above the body, a wax image of Caesar was raised, showing all twenty-three wounds, and turned slowly on a mechanism so that the whole crowd could see it.

465
00:45:36,638 --> 00:45:42,698
If the detail is true, it was one of the most effective pieces of political theatre in history.

466
00:45:44,998 --> 00:45:46,522
The crowd exploded.

467
00:45:46,902 --> 00:45:58,210
Instead of carrying the body to the pyre prepared on the Field of Mars, they built one right there in the Forum, from benches, tables, the judges' platforms, anything that would burn.

468
00:45:58,590 --> 00:46:01,638
Veterans threw their weapons into the flames.

469
00:46:02,018 --> 00:46:06,037
Musicians and actors tore off their costumes and threw them in.

470
00:46:06,417 --> 00:46:08,367
Women threw in their jewellery.

471
00:46:08,747 --> 00:46:11,548
Caesar was cremated in the heart of Rome.

472
00:46:13,848 --> 00:46:16,466
Then the mob went looking for the assassins.

473
00:46:16,846 --> 00:46:21,968
They carried burning brands to the houses of Brutus and Cassius, and were driven back.

474
00:46:22,348 --> 00:46:33,121
In the chaos they caught a man called Helvius Cinna, a poet and a friend of Caesar, and mistook him for Cornelius Cinna, one of Caesar's enemies who had praised the murder.

475
00:46:33,501 --> 00:46:35,551
They tore him apart in the street.

476
00:46:35,931 --> 00:46:39,076
Within weeks, Brutus and Cassius had left Rome.

477
00:46:39,456 --> 00:46:41,087
They would never return.

478
00:46:53,387 --> 00:47:04,668
When Caesar died, Gaius Octavius was across the Adriatic, at Apollonia, in what is now Albania, studying and training with the legions gathering for the Parthian war.

479
00:47:05,048 --> 00:47:07,458
He was eighteen, slight, often ill.

480
00:47:07,838 --> 00:47:11,139
His family begged him not to accept the inheritance.

481
00:47:11,519 --> 00:47:13,378
It was far too dangerous.

482
00:47:13,758 --> 00:47:17,592
He accepted it anyway, and took his adoptive father's name.

483
00:47:17,972 --> 00:47:21,402
From now on, he called himself Gaius Julius Caesar.

484
00:47:23,702 --> 00:47:26,572
Mark Antony did not take him seriously.

485
00:47:26,952 --> 00:47:32,384
Cicero later quoted him mocking Octavian as a boy who owed everything to a name.

486
00:47:32,764 --> 00:47:34,530
But the name was everything.

487
00:47:34,910 --> 00:47:37,031
Caesar's veterans flocked to him.

488
00:47:37,411 --> 00:47:47,620
He paid the three hundred sesterces of Caesar's will to the people out of his own pocket, and when Antony refused to hand over the money, he sold his own property to do it.

489
00:47:49,920 --> 00:47:58,253
In July of 44 BC, during games in Caesar's honour, a bright comet appeared in the sky and shone for seven days.

490
00:47:58,633 --> 00:48:07,249
Octavian himself, as Pliny the Elder records, said the people believed it was Caesar's soul being received among the immortal gods.

491
00:48:07,629 --> 00:48:10,746
He had a star placed on the head of Caesar's statues.

492
00:48:11,126 --> 00:48:16,405
Caesar was on his way to becoming a god, and his heir to being the son of a god.

493
00:48:18,705 --> 00:48:22,292
The next eighteen months were a blur of shifting alliances.

494
00:48:22,672 --> 00:48:26,580
Octavian first sided with the Senate against Antony.

495
00:48:26,960 --> 00:48:36,613
At the battle of Mutina, in April 43 BC, both consuls of the year were killed, and Octavian was left in command of their armies.

496
00:48:36,993 --> 00:48:40,284
He marched on Rome and made himself consul at nineteen.

497
00:48:40,664 --> 00:48:44,746
Then, to the Senate's horror, he made a deal with Antony and Lepidus.

498
00:48:47,046 --> 00:48:54,859
The three men met on a small river island near Bononia, modern Bologna, and divided the Roman world between them.

499
00:48:55,239 --> 00:48:58,705
Modern historians call it the Second Triumvirate.

500
00:48:59,085 --> 00:49:02,677
Unlike the first, it was made legal, for five years.

501
00:49:03,057 --> 00:49:05,300
And like Sulla, they published lists.

502
00:49:05,680 --> 00:49:15,525
According to Appian, some three hundred senators and two thousand wealthy equestrians were proscribed, their property seized, their heads brought in for a reward.

503
00:49:17,825 --> 00:49:20,779
The most famous name on the lists was Cicero's.

504
00:49:21,159 --> 00:49:27,739
Antony had never forgiven the series of brilliant speeches Cicero had delivered against him, the Philippics.

505
00:49:28,119 --> 00:49:35,128
On the seventh of December, 43 BC, soldiers caught the old orator at his villa near Formiae.

506
00:49:35,508 --> 00:49:38,640
He leaned out of his litter and offered them his neck.

507
00:49:39,020 --> 00:49:45,910
His head and his hands, the hands that had written the Philippics, were nailed up on the speaker's platform in the Forum.

508
00:49:48,210 --> 00:49:50,811
The assassins were already being hunted.

509
00:49:51,191 --> 00:49:57,815
Trebonius was the first to die, killed at Smyrna in early 43 by one of Caesar's partisans.

510
00:49:58,195 --> 00:50:08,428
Decimus Brutus, the man who had brought Caesar to the Senate, was abandoned by his army, captured by a Gallic chieftain, and executed on Antony's orders.

511
00:50:08,809 --> 00:50:17,440
And in January 42 BC, the Senate formally declared Julius Caesar a god: Divus Iulius, the deified Julius.

512
00:50:29,740 --> 00:50:41,516
Brutus and Cassius had gone east, to the provinces of Greece and Asia, where they raised armies of their own, some eighty thousand men, and squeezed the cities of the region for money.

513
00:50:41,896 --> 00:50:46,553
And Brutus did something that no Roman tyrant-killer had done before.

514
00:50:46,933 --> 00:50:48,218
He struck a coin.

515
00:50:48,598 --> 00:50:51,022
On one side, his own portrait.

516
00:50:51,402 --> 00:50:56,355
On the other, a cap of liberty between two daggers, and two words: EID MAR.

517
00:50:56,735 --> 00:50:58,429
The Ides of March.

518
00:51:00,729 --> 00:51:08,920
According to Plutarch, one night in his tent, as Brutus sat working by lamplight, a monstrous figure appeared beside him.

519
00:51:09,300 --> 00:51:11,172
Brutus asked who it was.

520
00:51:11,552 --> 00:51:14,872
The figure answered: I am your evil spirit, Brutus.

521
00:51:15,252 --> 00:51:16,784
You will see me at Philippi.

522
00:51:17,164 --> 00:51:21,510
Plutarch tells it as a story, and the modern reader can take it as one.

523
00:51:21,890 --> 00:51:26,082
But it shows how the ancient writers saw Brutus at the end: haunted.

524
00:51:28,382 --> 00:51:33,675
In October 42 BC, the armies met at Philippi, in northern Greece.

525
00:51:34,055 --> 00:51:37,617
Antony and Octavian had about nineteen legions.

526
00:51:37,997 --> 00:51:40,831
Brutus and Cassius had a similar number.

527
00:51:41,211 --> 00:51:45,771
In the first battle, Brutus broke Octavian's wing and stormed his camp.

528
00:51:46,151 --> 00:51:48,818
Octavian, who was ill, was not there.

529
00:51:49,198 --> 00:51:52,125
But on the other wing, Antony crushed Cassius.

530
00:51:54,425 --> 00:51:58,876
From his hill, Cassius could not see through the dust that Brutus had won.

531
00:51:59,256 --> 00:52:01,735
He sent an officer to find out.

532
00:52:02,115 --> 00:52:05,812
When he saw horsemen surround the man, he thought he had been captured.

533
00:52:06,192 --> 00:52:10,714
He went into his tent with his freedman Pindarus and ordered him to kill him.

534
00:52:11,094 --> 00:52:16,138
Plutarch says Cassius died by the same dagger he had used against Caesar.

535
00:52:18,438 --> 00:52:22,601
Three weeks later, in the second battle, Brutus's army broke.

536
00:52:22,981 --> 00:52:25,804
That night, with a few friends, he killed himself.

537
00:52:26,184 --> 00:52:29,947
Plutarch says he fell on his sword, which was held by a friend.

538
00:52:30,327 --> 00:52:34,370
Antony, finding the body, covered it with his own purple cloak.

539
00:52:36,670 --> 00:52:42,090
Before he died, according to Plutarch, Brutus said that he was happier than the victors.

540
00:52:42,470 --> 00:52:50,109
He was leaving behind, he said, a reputation for virtue that they would never have, for all their armies and their money.

541
00:52:50,489 --> 00:52:52,956
Whether he believed it, no one can say.

542
00:52:53,336 --> 00:53:01,255
When the body was found, Octavian ordered that the head be cut off and sent to Rome, to be thrown at the feet of Caesar's statue.

543
00:53:01,635 --> 00:53:02,909
It never arrived.

544
00:53:03,289 --> 00:53:07,190
According to Cassius Dio, it was lost in a storm at sea.

545
00:53:09,490 --> 00:53:13,244
With Philippi, the last army of the Republic was destroyed.

546
00:53:13,624 --> 00:53:22,444
Suetonius says that hardly any of the assassins outlived Caesar by more than three years, and that none of them died a natural death.

547
00:53:22,824 --> 00:53:26,347
Some died in battle, some at sea, some were executed.

548
00:53:26,727 --> 00:53:30,416
Some, like Cassius, turned the same daggers on themselves.

549
00:53:30,796 --> 00:53:33,716
It is an exaggeration, but not by much.

550
00:53:46,016 --> 00:53:48,678
The alliance of the victors did not last.

551
00:53:49,058 --> 00:53:51,055
Lepidus was pushed aside.

552
00:53:51,435 --> 00:53:55,929
Antony took the East, and Cleopatra, and Octavian took the West.

553
00:53:56,309 --> 00:54:01,456
In 31 BC their fleets met at Actium, off the coast of Greece.

554
00:54:01,836 --> 00:54:03,040
Octavian won.

555
00:54:03,420 --> 00:54:08,540
A year later, in Alexandria, Antony and Cleopatra took their own lives.

556
00:54:08,920 --> 00:54:13,999
Caesarion, the boy who may have been Caesar's son, was hunted down and killed.

557
00:54:14,379 --> 00:54:20,399
One of Octavian's advisers is said to have told him that too many Caesars was not a good thing.

558
00:54:22,699 --> 00:54:29,350
In 27 BC, Octavian made a grand show of handing power back to the Senate and the people.

559
00:54:29,730 --> 00:54:34,097
The grateful Senate gave him a new name: Augustus, the revered one.

560
00:54:34,477 --> 00:54:38,244
He kept the consuls, the Senate, the elections and the laws.

561
00:54:38,624 --> 00:54:41,890
He never called himself king, and never dictator.

562
00:54:42,270 --> 00:54:44,966
He was simply princeps, the first citizen.

563
00:54:45,346 --> 00:54:50,741
Behind the old forms, one man ruled Rome until his death, forty years later.

564
00:54:51,121 --> 00:54:53,516
Every ruler after him did the same.

565
00:54:53,896 --> 00:54:55,587
The Republic was over.

566
00:54:57,887 --> 00:54:58,893
So who won?

567
00:54:59,273 --> 00:55:02,110
The assassins wanted to save the Republic.

568
00:55:02,490 --> 00:55:12,169
They killed the man they thought was a tyrant, and within twenty years the Republic had been replaced by an emperor more powerful than Caesar had ever been.

569
00:55:12,549 --> 00:55:18,451
Caesar's heir carried his name, completed his building projects, and worshipped him as a god.

570
00:55:18,831 --> 00:55:23,332
The daggers of the Ides of March did not stop the end of the Republic.

571
00:55:23,712 --> 00:55:25,724
In many ways, they finished it.

572
00:55:28,024 --> 00:55:32,057
And yet the idea the conspirators died for did not die with them.

573
00:55:32,437 --> 00:55:37,733
Two thousand years later, the founders of new republics read Cicero and Plutarch.

574
00:55:38,113 --> 00:55:40,935
They argued about Caesar and about Brutus.

575
00:55:41,315 --> 00:55:42,630
Was he a tyrant?

576
00:55:43,010 --> 00:55:45,448
Was killing him murder, or a duty?

577
00:55:45,827 --> 00:55:48,810
Those questions have never quite been settled.

578
00:55:51,110 --> 00:55:55,747
Caesar himself left no account of his own aims in those final months.

579
00:55:56,127 --> 00:55:58,626
His enemies said he wanted a crown.

580
00:55:59,006 --> 00:56:01,273
His friends said he wanted order.

581
00:56:01,653 --> 00:56:08,271
The truth may be that he did not think the Republic could be saved in its old form, and he did not much care.

582
00:56:08,651 --> 00:56:16,574
Suetonius quotes a hostile writer who claimed that Caesar called the Republic nothing, a mere name without body or form.

583
00:56:16,954 --> 00:56:19,441
If he really said it, he was half right.

584
00:56:19,821 --> 00:56:22,231
The forms survived him for centuries.

585
00:56:22,611 --> 00:56:24,334
The thing itself did not.

586
00:56:26,634 --> 00:56:29,011
Some things we can say with confidence.

587
00:56:29,391 --> 00:56:34,927
The murder took place not on the Capitol, as Shakespeare has it, but in the Curia of Pompey.

588
00:56:35,307 --> 00:56:38,015
Caesar's last words were not et tu, Brute.

589
00:56:38,395 --> 00:56:45,327
Spurinna's warning was real enough to be remembered, but beware the Ides of March is a line from a play.

590
00:56:45,707 --> 00:56:54,058
And Caesar was killed not by a crowd of strangers, but by men he had pardoned, promoted and, in one case, named in his will.

591
00:56:56,358 --> 00:56:58,487
His name outlived everyone.

592
00:56:58,867 --> 00:57:05,340
Caesar became the title of the Roman emperors, and then of the German Kaiser and the Russian Tsar.

593
00:57:05,720 --> 00:57:07,706
His month is still July.

594
00:57:08,086 --> 00:57:11,813
His calendar, with one correction, still divides our year.

595
00:57:14,113 --> 00:57:26,332
And in the centre of modern Rome, in a sunken square called Largo di Torre Argentina, among the ruins of four Republican temples, you can still see part of the base of the hall where it happened.

596
00:57:26,712 --> 00:57:28,730
Cats sleep on the old stones.

597
00:57:29,110 --> 00:57:37,004
And in the Forum, at the altar built where his body burned, people still leave flowers every year, on the fifteenth of March.

